  grid: 30x30 [observed]
  colors: 4 distinct -- bg (most frequent), wall_color, door_color, marker_color
  orientation: vertical or horizontal (all walls share one orientation)
  walls:
    count: 3-4 parallel full-span lines (each spans entire grid height or width)
    composition: wall_color everywhere except one 3-pixel door segment per wall
    spacing: >=2 cells between adjacent walls; >=1 cell from grid edge to nearest wall
  doors:
    shape: 3-pixel straight bar, same orientation as wall, centered in a wall
    color: door_color (pixel count < wall_color pixel count)
    position: door center >=3 cells from nearest grid edge along wall axis
  markers:
    count: exactly 2 pixels of marker_color
    placement: on opposite outer sides of the wall set (both in bg-only corridors)
    edge_dist: 1-3 cells from nearest grid edge [observed]
  output_rule: |
    Draw marker_color path from start marker through each door center (in wall order)
    to end marker. At each wall: go along corridor to 1 cell before wall, turn along
    wall to door row/col, cross through door center (replacing it with marker_color),
    then continue. Door flank pixels unchanged. Only bg/door-center cells are painted.

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first (path-first)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid 30x30, orientation (H or V), 4 distinct colors for bg/wall/door/marker.
    2. Choose N walls (3-4). Place wall positions with gaps >=2 between adjacent walls
       and >=1 from each edge.
    3. For each wall, pick a door center position >=3 from each end of the wall axis.
    4. Place start marker in the first outer corridor, end marker in the last outer
       corridor, both with edge_dist 1-3.
    5. Trace the path through door centers to verify no self-intersection and all
       segments stay on bg cells. This is guaranteed by construction since each
       approach column/row is unique (one per wall).
    6. Fill walls with wall_color, doors with door_color, markers with marker_color,
       rest with bg. Output is the input with the traced path painted in marker_color.


